Synopsys Synthesis
==================

This directory contains the following sub-directories:

1. work       - contains the intermediate files produced by the Synopsys
                'analyze' command.
2. db         - contains the synthesised blocks in Synopsys .db format.
3. cell_lib   - this needs to contain a link or copy of the cell library.db
4. log        - contains log files of the commands run during synthesis
                for each block.
5. netlist    - contains the synthesised netlist for the design.
6. scr_common - contains Synopsys scripts which are common to several designs.
7. scr_kmi    - contains Synopsys scripts which are specific to the KMI design.
8. scr_scan   - contains Synopsys scripts for scan insertion of the KMI,
                reading in the pre-compiled .db file in db/ directory.
9. report     - contains reports written out from synopsys. 

Before synthesising the block, scripts will need to be modified to select
the required cell library and define directory paths:

1. The setup script, setup.scr in the sub-directory ./scr_common, defines
   the following key parameters
     - the cell library to target for synthesis
     - library cells not to be used for synthesis
     - location of the Synopsys read and write cache

   The above will need modifying for the choice of cell library
   and specific application details.
   Refer to comments in the setup.scr script file for other
   parameters that might also need modifying.

2. The global script, global.scr in ./scr_common 
   uses parameters from the cell library settings file, cb25v2.1settings.scr
   These parameters need modifying to target a different cell library, to define:
     - worst and best case conditions of the cell library
     - default cell drive and load strength

3. The script files cb25dontuse.scr in scr_common defines cells that should
   not be used for synthesis.
   The script cb25scanuse.scr in scr_scan, define cells that should be used
   for scan replacement of sequential cells.

4. The directory cell_lib should contain links (or the files) for the
   target technology cell library, in Synopsys format.
   For example:
   ln -s /eda/design_kits/avanti/cb25/v2.1/synopsys/cb25os163/1997.01/models/cb25os163_max.db
   ln -s /eda/design_kits/avanti/cb25/v2.1/synopsys/cb25os163/1997.01/models/cb25os163_typ.db
   ln -s /eda/design_kits/avanti/cb25/v2.1/synopsys/cb25os163/1997.01/models/cb25os163_min.db
   ln -s /eda/design_kits/avanti/cb25/v2.1/synopsys/cb25os163/1997.01/icons/cb25os163.sdb

5. The master compile script, kmi.cmd is located in the ./scr_kmi directory.
   This script controls the synthesis process.
   No scan insertion is performed by this script.

   The scripts require some environment variables to be declared before
   starting synthesis, and these are described below.

   The script variables hdl and hdl_out are assigned to UNIX environment
   variables $HDL_IN and $HDL_OUT, and these must be declared before
   beginning synthesis.
   For example
     setenv HDL_IN  vhdl      and   setenv HDL_OUT vhdl
   or
     setenv HDL_IN  verilog   and   setenv HDL_OUT verilog

   The variable 'hdl' selects the hdl source being compiled.
   The variable 'hdl_out' controls the output format of the netlist.

   Alternatively, the script can be amended to define hdl and hdl_out
   without use of environment variables. If so desired, it is allowable
   to use VHDL source and output in Verilog format, or vice-versa.

   The variable design_root specifies the root directory path of the design
   using the environment variable $PERIPH.
   If environment variables are not required then enter a fixed path
   for design_root.

6. The timing parameters for the AMBA APB interface are defined by
   apb_params.scr and the constraints set in apb_slave.scr in ./scr_common.

   Depending on the design style of the APB bridge (whether logic occurs
   before or after registers) the parameters may need changing.
   For example, where the APB bridge has registered outputs the maximum
   input delay can be reduced resulting in a smaller area requirement.

7. An additional compile script kmi_scan.cmd in scr_scan/ allows
   scan insertion of the design as a post-synthesis process.
   The compile script kmi.cmd must be run first to obtain a .db file
   (in the db/ directory/) which has mapped and optimised the design
   to the chosen cell library.
   The kmi_scan.cmd compile script may then be used to replace 
   sequential cells by their scan equivalents from the cell library.

8. To synthesise the block, change to the synopsys_ref/ directory
   (for example: cd $PERIPH/synopsys_ref)
   and type:

   dc_shell -checkout "VHDL-Compiler" < scr_kmi/kmi.cmd > log/kmi_vhdl.log

   or

   dc_shell -checkout "HDL-Compiler" < scr_kmi/kmi.cmd > log/kmi_verilog.log

   Note: For use with design_analyser, some commands may require
     the '\' line continuation character to be removed.

9. To scan insert the design, change to the synopsys_ref/ directory and type:

  dc_shell -checkout "VHDL-Compiler Test-Compiler" < scr_scan/kmi_scan.cmd > log/kmi_scan_vhdl.log

  or

  dc_shell -checkout "HDL-Compiler Test-Compiler" < scr_scan/kmi_scan.cmd > log/kmi_scan_verilog.log

  NOTE: The previous step above must be run first
        to create the appropriate .db file in the db/ directory.

/**************************** End of README *********************************/
